"""Differential tests against real vixie-cron at daylight saving transitions.

Why this file exists
--------------------
Every claim cronnext makes about DST -- skip the spring gap, run the repeated
hour once -- was originally asserted against cronnext's own expectations.
``tests/vixie_oracle.py`` ports only vixie's *day* rule and has no notion of a
timezone, so nothing ever checked the wall clock arithmetic against vixie.
``tests/naive.py`` could not help either: it shares ``CronExpr.day_matches``
with the library, so it is a consistency check, not an oracle.

These expectations were captured from vixie-cron 3.0pl1 built from its own
sources, calling ``load_entry()`` from ``entry.c`` and reproducing ``cron.c``'s
``cron_tick()`` predicate verbatim, with the epoch handed to ``localtime_r()``
exactly as ``cron_tick()`` hands ``TargetTime`` to ``localtime()``.  Driving
that binary under a real TZ therefore exercises genuine vixie semantics, DST
included.  The verdicts are frozen in ``VIXIE_FIRINGS`` below, so the suite
needs no oracle binary and none ships in the repository.

What the capture found
----------------------
Across 11 zones and 6 years, at every offset change in those ranges, over the
half-open window (lo, hi]::

    37 284 vixie firings
    GAP  transitions: 616 expression/windows exact,   0 diverging
    FOLD transitions: 284 expression/windows exact, 321 diverging

Every divergence is the same one: cronnext reports an ambiguous local time
once, vixie runs it twice.  Not one gap case diverged, including
Australia/Lord_Howe's 30 minute gap and Pacific/Apia's 24 hour jump, where a
whole calendar day never happened.

Upstream vixie's main loop is::

    cron_tick(&database);
    TargetTime += 60;

``TargetTime`` is a ``time_t``, so the loop advances by 60 absolute seconds.  A
repeated wall clock hour is traversed twice and an entry whose time falls inside
it matches on both passes.

The fold is also where the two vixie flavours part company, so this file does
not claim cronnext matches "vixie" full stop.  Debian's patched cron.c calls
``find_jobs(timeRunning, &database, TRUE, FALSE)`` when DST ends, passing
``doNonWild = FALSE``, with the comment that fixed-time jobs "probably have
already run, and should not be repeated", while upstream steps blindly by 60
seconds.  cronnext's single run matches Debian's intent and departs from
upstream's.  See the README.

The window is half-open on both sides, ``(lo, hi]``: ``next_after`` is strictly
after its argument, so feeding the oracle ``lo`` itself would describe a
different interval and put a phantom mismatch on every boundary.  An earlier
version of the capture harness did exactly that and invented GAP failures.
"""

